.TH GRPALETTE 2
.SH NAME
grpalette: set, setcolour, value, values, table, images, shade, mix, dither, ramp \- the TempleOS colour registers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "draw.m";
include "grpalette.m";
grpal := load Grpalette Grpalette->PATH;

grpal->init();

grpal->set(Grpalette->EGA);
grpal->setcolour(i, rgba);
which := grpal->loaded();
rgba := grpal->value(i);
cols := grpal->values();
cols = grpal->table(Grpalette->RICH);
s := grpal->name(i);

ink := grpal->images(display);

rgba = grpal->shade(rgba, lit);
rgba = grpal->mix(a, b, t);
rgba = grpal->dither(a, b);
shades := grpal->ramp(display, rgba, n);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
TempleOS draws in sixteen colours, and a colour is an
.I index
into
.BR gr.palette ,
not a colour in itself:
.B GrPaletteColorSet
rewrites a register underneath a drawing that has already been made, which
is what
.IR temple (1)'s
.B palette
demo is about.
.I Grpalette
is that register file.
.PP
The sixteen indices are named
.BR BLACK ,
.BR BLUE ,
.BR GREEN ,
.BR CYAN ,
.BR RED ,
.BR PURPLE ,
.BR BROWN ,
.BR LTGRAY ,
.BR DKGRAY ,
.BR LTBLUE ,
.BR LTGREEN ,
.BR LTCYAN ,
.BR LTRED ,
.BR LTPURPLE ,
.B YELLOW
and
.BR WHITE ,
in that order, and
.B COLORS
is 16.
Index
.I i
and index
.IB i +8
are the same colour dim and bright, which is the pairing TempleOS's
lighting code relies on.
.SS Palettes
.B set
loads all sixteen registers from one of three named palettes.
.TP
.B EGA
The sixteen TempleOS boots with, byte for byte.
This is the palette that was written out as a literal in each port before
this module existed, so a claim that a port renders what its HolyC
rendered is still checkable: set
.B EGA
and the pixels are the ones that were there.
.TP
.B RICH
The same sixteen meanings, spent on a display that is not an EGA DAC.
Every entry keeps its hue and its place in the dim/bright pair, so a port
draws exactly what it drew and only looks better doing it.
.B BLACK
and
.B WHITE
are identical to
.BR EGA 's,
deliberately: ports mix palette black with
.B Draw->Black
in the same frame and a near-black would show as a seam.
.TP
.B GREY
0xFFFF*\fIi\fP/15 in every channel \(em the ramp
.B Palette.HC
writes over the registers.
.PP
The palette a program starts in comes from
.BR $grpalette :
.BR ega ,
.B grey
or, unset,
.BR RICH .
Each
.B load
of the module gets its own register file, so one program cannot disturb
another's.
.PP
.B setcolour
writes a single register, as
.B GrPaletteColorSet
does; after it,
.B loaded
returns \-1, because no named palette is in any more.
.B value
returns one register as a
.B Draw
RGBA word and wraps its index to four bits;
.B values
returns a copy of all sixteen;
.B table
returns a named palette without loading it; and
.B name
returns the index's TempleOS name.
.B images
allocates one
.B Draw->Image
per register against
.IR display .
.SS Colours the registers do not hold
The remaining functions produce colours that are not in the sixteen, which
is what the original could not do and faked with dither.
.PP
.B shade
scales a colour by
.IR lit :
0 is black, 1 is the colour, 2 is white, and past full brightness the
excess goes towards white rather than clipping to it.
This is the term
.IR draw3d (2)'s
.B fillpoly3
already takes.
.B mix
interpolates between two colours,
.I a
at
.I t
= 0 and
.I b
at
.I t
= 1;
.B dither
is the midpoint, which is what a
.B ROPF_DITHER
checkerboard of two registers averages to.
.B ramp
returns
.I n
images running dark to light through a colour, the middle one being the
colour itself.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/grpalette.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR draw-image (2),
.IR draw3d (2),
.IR grpalettetest (1),
.IR temple (1)
.SH BUGS
A colour is a 32-bit RGBA word, but this tree's
.B int
is 64 bits wide at run time while the compiler types a literal of
16r80000000 or more as
.BR big .
A computed white is therefore 4294967295 and a written one is
.B int
16rFFFFFFFF, which is \-1: they draw the same, because only the low 32
bits reach the display, and they compare unequal.
Every value this module returns is sign-extended so that it compares equal
to the literal it stands for, but a caller doing its own colour arithmetic
must do the same.
